WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.280
How does "The Batman's" explosive finale stack up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.040
to "Batman &amp; Robin's" pun-filled&nbsp;
finale? Does "Batman v. Superman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.080
Dawn of Justice" wear the kryptonite crown when&nbsp;
it comes to Bat-finales? Holy climaxes, Batman!

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.040
There aren't a whole lot of people out there&nbsp;
who would argue that Joel Schumacher's 1997&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.480
film "Batman &amp; Robin" was a masterpiece. The&nbsp;
bloated, flashy sequel to the 1995 hit "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.720
Forever" dialed the silliness way up to an&nbsp;
unprecedented degree. While the television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.240
series from 1966 starring Adam West was also&nbsp;
very goofy, the humor was intentional. None of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.360
the jokes in "Batman &amp; Robin" land and the rest&nbsp;
of it is basically an amusement park stunt show.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.920
The climax of the film is no different. With&nbsp;
Gotham City frozen, it's up to Batman, Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.000
and Batgirl to save the day. While that&nbsp;
isn't a terrible premise for a fun and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.760
campy adventure film, it's so noisy and&nbsp;
cluttered that you can't appreciate any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.840
of the heroics that we're supposed to find so&nbsp;
exciting. On top of that, the characters are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.240
devoid of charisma and depth that we can't even&nbsp;
force ourselves to care what happens to them.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.560
With a few changes in the dialogue, casting, and&nbsp;
tone, this could have been a loving big-budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.600
tribute to the 1966 series. Instead, it&nbsp;
comes across as a cynical toy commercial.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.200
"You're about to become compost!"

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.560
After the dark and twisted "Batman Returns," the&nbsp;
franchise took a notably more family-friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.160
approach with its next installment,&nbsp;
"Batman Forever." The film is big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.280
bold, and incredibly uneven. While it contains&nbsp;
some staggering imagery, kinetic cinematography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.600
and a fun performance from Jim Carrey as The&nbsp;
Riddler, there isn't much of a story happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.320
The action set pieces are linked&nbsp;
together by scenes that hint at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.160
interesting ideas and possibilities&nbsp;
that never get a chance to develop.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:34.720
"Nice bike."

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.040
"Hang out at a lot of biker bars, Bruce?"

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.960
Bruce coming to terms with his own rage over&nbsp;
the murder of his parents in order to help the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.120
grieving Dick Grayson cope with his own tragedy&nbsp;
could have led to a thrilling conclusion, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.280
the heroes finally suit up and work together.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, the conflict between them is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.720
limited to a few arguments with no substance. So&nbsp;
as huge and visually riveting as Batman and Robin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.440
facing off against The Riddler and Two-Face may be&nbsp;
on a purely cinematic level, the lack of any real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.360
emotional resonance leaves this otherwise&nbsp;
mostly entertaining film feeling hollow.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.880
Tim Burton's second film about the Dark Knight,&nbsp;
"Batman Returns," has all the ingredients of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.440
masterpiece. For one thing, it features an&nbsp;
incredible cast playing iconic characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.920
Also, Burton had just come off of a major creative&nbsp;
victory with the stunning and sentimental modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.400
fairytale "Edward Scissorhands," suggesting&nbsp;
his next project would be his magnum opus.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.720
There are elements of genius here, but just&nbsp;
like "Batman Forever," the story linking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.360
them together is paper-thin. The tragedy of&nbsp;
Oswald Cobblepot seeking revenge on a city that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.360
rejected him could have been the perfect&nbsp;
motivation for a truly maniacal villain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.720
Bruce and Selina's doomed romance could&nbsp;
have set up one of the all-time greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.600
love stories. But none of it leads to anything.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.040
"Hey stud. I thought we had something together."

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.500
"We do.”

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.680
Most of the character motivations are as murky&nbsp;
as sewer water, making it difficult to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.560
why anything happening even matters. This makes it&nbsp;
virtually impossible to get invested in the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.680
climax. All the primary players are together,&nbsp;
but we're not quite sure what brought them there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.120
Still, the Penguin's death is truly unsettling,&nbsp;
as is Selina's even stranger murder of Max Shreck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.120
If nothing else, you remember what happens,&nbsp;
even if you're not sure why they happen.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.320
Some people may consider this a little high for&nbsp;
the original "Batman" feature film. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.400
many fans consider it a borderline parody of&nbsp;
the character. However, the film is closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.560
to the style and substance of the comics of&nbsp;
the time than many fans might like to admit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.440
and at least the tone is&nbsp;
consistent throughout. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.920
unlike some earlier entries on this list,&nbsp;
the humor is intentional and actually funny.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:40.400
"Listen to these riddles. Tell me if you interpret&nbsp;
them as I do. 1. What has yellow skin and writes?"

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.000
"A ballpoint banana!"

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:42.560
"Right."

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.640
The movie is almost the greatest hits of&nbsp;
the television series. The Penguin, Riddler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.600
Joker, and Catwoman have joined forces for world&nbsp;
domination. Through a series of fiendish plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.360
this unholy alliance gets close to&nbsp;
accomplishing their dastardly goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.760
but Batman and Robin never give up. With&nbsp;
lots of comedy, exciting plot twists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.240
and a splash of romance, this film succeeds&nbsp;
in delivering a crowd-pleasing experience.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.400
The finale even features an all-out battle&nbsp;
royale aboard a submarine, with Batman and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.040
Robin clocking henchmen left and right, sending&nbsp;
them into the sea. At one point, Batman takes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.320
a series of thugs while cradling a cat in one arm,&nbsp;
before sending it off in a tiny, inflatable raft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.160
It's bright, colorful, intentionally&nbsp;
silly, and the ending does not disappoint.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.840
After the brilliant heights achieved in 2008's&nbsp;
"The Dark Knight," there was no way Christopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.320
Nolan was going to top himself. That being said,&nbsp;
"The Dark Knight Rises" makes a valiant attempt at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.920
doing just that. Its scale is bigger, the tone is&nbsp;
relentless, and Bane — though not as charismatic a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.120
character as the Joker — is a complex adversary&nbsp;
capable of pushing Batman to the very brink.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.360
The problem lies in the decision to make this&nbsp;
a concluding chapter of a trilogy, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.600
the Batman's next adventure. After setting&nbsp;
up this fascinating universe in the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.240
installments, we were denied the chance to see new&nbsp;
interpretations of other fan-favorite characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.960
This Batman story was ending just as it&nbsp;
began. This led to an overly complex and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.520
confusing plot that had to both establish new&nbsp;
characters and tie them into Batman's origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.360
instead of standing on their own.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.120
While the film is still epic in scope and&nbsp;
packed with phenomenal action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.880
story inconsistencies and sudden&nbsp;
revelations weigh the whole thing down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.200
Still, the image of Batman possibly flying&nbsp;
off to his death to save the city is powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.640
and the suggestion that John Blake&nbsp;
(aka Robin) is going to take up his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.080
mantle allows the world to live on in&nbsp;
the imaginations of fans everywhere.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.520
The first cinematic pairing of Batman and&nbsp;
Superman may not have been what everyone expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.920
but it did result in some pretty great moments&nbsp;
that almost outshine the film's weaknesses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.920
The ending is a prime example of all the&nbsp;
things that do and do not work in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.120
For instance, the confusing plot&nbsp;
orchestrated by Lex Luthor to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.960
Batman and Superman fight to the death&nbsp;
is conveniently resolved when Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.840
inadvertently informs Batman that&nbsp;
their mothers share the same name.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.080
"You're letting him kill Martha."

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.240
Also, Superman's sacrifice is&nbsp;
both overblown and unnecessary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.240
We just met this Superman in the previous film,&nbsp;
and we're already being asked to mourn his death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.920
when we've had very little time to get to&nbsp;
know him. It's treated like an enormously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.560
tragic moment but it comes practically out of&nbsp;
nowhere. Conversely, the inclusion of Wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.200
Woman gives us the chill-inducing moment where we&nbsp;
finally get to see DC's trinity stand together.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.120
Just like the rest of the film, many of the&nbsp;
climax's details are muddled and strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.320
but the simple excitement of having&nbsp;
three classic heroes joining forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.720
to defeat a common enemy is absolutely thrilling.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.800
Matt Reeves' attempt to reconnect Batman to his&nbsp;
noir detective roots has, appropriately enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.560
a dark ending that feels like something out&nbsp;
of Dashiell Hammett or Raymond Chandler.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.160
Gotham has been largely destroyed, the&nbsp;
result of Riddler's plan to detonate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.520
bombs near the seawalls surrounding the&nbsp;
city, sending rushing waters through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.600
the streets and nearly killing hundreds&nbsp;
of citizens gathered at a local arena.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.600
Thankfully, Batman and Catwoman were there&nbsp;
to help out, battling Riddler-wannabes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.360
attempting to anonymously amplify the&nbsp;
supervillain's destructive efforts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.760
Although they save characters like Bella Real and&nbsp;
the late Mayor Mitchell's son, anybody you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.320
reasonably call a "good guy" in this film limps&nbsp;
into the shadows at the end, licking their wounds.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.280
Catwoman, realizing Batman is forever linked&nbsp;
with Gotham, leaves the city. Bruce Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.560
newly burdened by realizations about his&nbsp;
beloved parents that make his mission of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.080
vengeance far more muddled, says in voiceover:&nbsp;
"The city is angry, scared ... like me."

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.080
With many of Gotham's most powerful men&nbsp;
on both sides now dead, opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.080
seem to be opening up for folks like&nbsp;
the Penguin. Riddler, now in prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.320
has a next-door neighbor at Arkham who&nbsp;
speaks of clowns and a "comeback story."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.320
If nothing else, the end of "The Batman"&nbsp;
seems to be setting up one heckuva sequel.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.920
"The Dark Knight" is probably considered the&nbsp;
best film on this list. It picked up the reins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.160
of "Batman Begins" and built an even bigger and&nbsp;
menacing world than before. Of course, Heath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.960
Ledger's performance as the Joker was instantly&nbsp;
legendary and remains a major contributing factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.280
to the film's success, but "The Dark Knight"&nbsp;
is still an epic by any and every measure.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.840
Which is why the end is such a letdown.&nbsp;
Not to say that it's bad by any means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.560
it's just that the rest of the film to this point&nbsp;
had been so dynamic and spectacular that the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:04.960
of waiting to see if criminals on a boat will&nbsp;
choose to destroy another boat doesn't measure up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.160
Also, we've seen Batman and&nbsp;
the Joker face off on top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.640
a building before, and it was much&nbsp;
more emotionally gratifying in 1989.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.720
Yes, Harvey Dent's turn to evil following&nbsp;
the murder of Rachel Dawes is tragic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.480
and the decision to let Batman take the rap&nbsp;
for his crimes perfectly demonstrates what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.640
makes him a great hero, but his defeat&nbsp;
of the Joker falls a little flat. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.880
is an oversimplification of the ending, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.360
but one can't help and wonder what happened to the&nbsp;
amazing momentum built up in the first two acts.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:37.760
The 1989 release of Tim Burton's "Batman" was the&nbsp;
introduction to the concept of a "dark" Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.760
for many audiences. While comic book readers&nbsp;
had been well aware of the character's many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.960
gothic exploits, your average moviegoer probably&nbsp;
just knew the Adam West Batman up to that point.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:51.280
The film is tonally inconsistent, with&nbsp;
a serviceable — if underwhelming — plot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.680
But it's all elevated by its ending. While the&nbsp;
rest of the film is certainly entertaining and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:59.840
still worth a re-watch today, it's the emotional&nbsp;
impact of the ending that allows the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.080
to stand tall as still being one of the very&nbsp;
best cinematic outings for the caped crusader.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.400
"You killed my parents."

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.120
"What? What are you talking about?"

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.320
"I made you, you made me first."

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.080
When it's revealed that the Joker was&nbsp;
the man who gunned down his parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.000
Batman pursues the mad clown through&nbsp;
Gotham City and to the top of a church.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.800
From here on out, Batman's mission isn't just&nbsp;
about protecting his city — he's out for revenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.520
Against the shadowy backdrop&nbsp;
of the towering cathedral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:34.320
Joker tries everything to prevent Batman from&nbsp;
seeking justice, but it is ultimately futile.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.040
Despite the fact that this confrontation, and the&nbsp;
motivations behind it, doesn't exactly honor the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.920
comics, all is forgiven, because the final moments&nbsp;
are so emotionally and narratively satisfying.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.480
"Batman Begins" is so different from&nbsp;
any of the films that came before that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.560
it's hard to imagine it was released a&nbsp;
mere eight years after "Batman &amp; Robin."

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.800
The next two films in this trilogy would take this&nbsp;
grounded reality even further, to the point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.680
they almost cease to be Batman movies and become&nbsp;
movies that happen to feature Batman. "Begins"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.760
however, is still holding onto some of the&nbsp;
more outrageous elements of Batman's comic book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.880
origins. The third act, with Scarecrow's nightmare&nbsp;
toxin causing horrific hallucinations and the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.600
of Gotham tearing itself apart, plays out like&nbsp;
something right out of the pages of a comic book.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.480
There are innocent citizens losing their minds&nbsp;
and a horse blowing flames from its nose — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.560
is absolute chaos, and Batman is using&nbsp;
everything he has to stop it from getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:29.680
worse. The most rewarding moment, though, has&nbsp;
to be Batman's final showdown with Ra's al Ghul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.040
The entire film sees Bruce trying to prove that&nbsp;
murder isn't justice. So when he tells Ra's

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:36.720
"I won't kill you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:44.320
But I don't have to save you."

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.120
It's a brilliant moment that makes&nbsp;
you want to stand up and cheer.

